# Pseudo-Brain

Pseudo-Brain is a standalone research project for a single, continuously
running sensorimotor model with a persistent world state and many parallel
latent microthoughts, scaling into a general-purpose cognitive agent
([PLAN.md §43](./PLAN.md#43-long-term-research-direction-general-purpose-language-tool-use--long-horizon-agent)).

The historical Python package name remains `irene_brain` so that immutable
training releases and checkpoints remain verifiable. New project work lives
under `Projects/Pseudo-Brain`; the separate Irene application and personality
data are not dependencies of this project.

The current campaign, in plain language, is
[CURRENT_WORK.md](../CURRENT_WORK.md). How to freeze, register, and launch is
[docs/OPERATOR_GUIDE.md](./docs/OPERATOR_GUIDE.md).
The current project blueprint is [PLAN.md](./PLAN.md).
Current implementation status is [STATUS.md](./STATUS.md).

This project is intentionally separate from Irene's turn-based voice/screen
pipeline and chat-personality supervision. Neither is training data for the
sensorimotor model.

Phase 0A provides a dependency-free deterministic core: generic controls,
canonical observations, a branchable pixel world, exact replay, counterfactual
rollouts, leakage-safe records, a fake-clock continuous driver, resource-policy
guards, experiment manifests, and one-brain compliance checks.

The live and recorded paths materialize the same resolved `ModelObservation`:
pixels, optional audio/text, relative timing, dropped-frame count, and the last
control actually applied. File references, current/future actions, rewards, and
privileged environment state are excluded by construction. Real-time catch-up
is bounded and faults return the controller to neutral.

Phase 1 now includes an opt-in PyTorch implementation of the single shared
thought-field model, a lazy million-transition procedural dataset, structured
307-channel action training, deterministic validation, mixed-precision training,
and integrity-checked checkpoint/resume. PyTorch remains lazily imported: merely
importing the Phase 0 package starts no accelerator runtime.

Training never starts automatically. The checked-in CUDA configurations are
for explicit offline execution on the DGX Spark; see
[DGX_SPARK_TRAINING.md](./docs/DGX_SPARK_TRAINING.md). The one-seed reference
qualification and untouched-TEST rules are frozen in
[RCQ_V2_PROTOCOL.md](./docs/RCQ_V2_PROTOCOL.md). Screen capture, physical input
injection, and background training remain disabled unless separately and
explicitly armed.

New DGX releases and runs use `~/projects/pseudo-brain` and the version-2
workspace marker. The old `~/projects/irene-brain` tree is retained in place as
read-only historical storage and is accessible only through explicit legacy
status/artifact retrieval switches.

The first bounded smoke and full-thesis canary completed successfully. The
first procedural bootstrap was deliberately stopped after its step-100 gate
exposed an all-directions action-loss shortcut; the objective and decisive
metrics were corrected and locally verified. The fresh immutable successor
then failed its bounded 100-step action-learning gate with near-zero logits, so
longer training remains blocked. See the
[first DGX run record](./docs/runs/2026-08-16-first-dgx-run.md) for immutable
release, checkpoint hashes, measured losses, and the live-control boundary.
The successor [corrected Stage A gate record](./docs/runs/2026-08-16-corrected-stagea-gate.md)
records the clean 100-step failure and the controlled action-path diagnostic
that must pass before longer training.
The matched [action overfit A/B record](./docs/runs/2026-08-16-action-overfit-ab.md)
shows that the action path can learn and selects the calibrated objective for
the broad held-out gate. The registered 500-step
[Stage A continuation gate](./docs/runs/2026-08-16-stagea-continuation-gate.md)
then improved late but validly failed its held-out behavioral thresholds. Its
matched [constant-schedule control](./docs/runs/2026-08-16-stagea-constant-gate.md)
improved exact held-out actions from `51/96` to `72/96`, reduced movement false
positives from 22 to 8, and removed opposite-direction conflicts, but still
failed the frozen `80/96` policy gate and value-loss bound. The 1,000-step
bootstrap and causal ablation remain blocked while matched baselines and a
larger untouched evaluation are built.
The preregistered [causal thought ablation](./docs/CAUSAL_THOUGHT_ABLATION.md)
tests only whether the complete final thought tensor beneficially affects
movement decisions on the fixed Stage-A slice; it does not certify slot
specialization or human-like thought.

From the `Pseudo-Brain` project root, run the CPU-only play-safe tests with:

~~~powershell
powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\brain\scripts\run_play_safe_tests.ps1
~~~
